WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:06.420
WINSTON CHANG: So this is in,
again, in the examples page.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:09.470
And if we scroll down,
there's a section

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:11.490
that demonstrates
different types of outputs.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.880
And so one of the
outputs is plot output.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.400
And this is a really
basic example, once again.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.220
So our UI is very similar.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.650
We have an input slider.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:25.220
And this time, we
have an output plot.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.200
And it's named plot.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.450
But just for clarity,
I'll call it myplot

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:33.230
just so that's not confusing.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:35.030
Now for this example,
we also needed

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:40.260
to import matplotlib and numpy
to generate some random data.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.370
So the code is pretty similar
to what we had before.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.470
We have this output decorator.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:48.830
And we have a render plot
decorator, this time,

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:50.000
instead of render text.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.890
And it has some alt
text which will display

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:53.833
if you hover over the plot.

00:00:53.833 --> 00:00:55.250
And then we have
the function that

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.728
actually generates the plot.

00:00:57.728 --> 00:00:59.270
Now, these first
two lines here, this

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.720
is just some numpy code to
generate random numbers.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.230
And then this is a
matplotlib histogram.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:07.700
So we just say plt.hist.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.790
We give it the data
that we generated.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.680
And for this
particular function,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.460
the second parameter is how
many bins are in the histogram.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.580
So that's basically like how
wide each of these bars is.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:26.990
So if I make it five,
there's five of these bars.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:29.600
If I make it 100,
there's 100 of them.

00:01:32.975 --> 00:01:34.610
And that's all that
it really takes

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:39.530
to create a plot in Shiny.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:40.910
You just say render.plot.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:43.940
And then if you're
using matplotlib,

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.760
you can say plt.hist.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:52.460
And Shiny also currently
supports seaborn and plotnine.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.770
And it also supports
other plotting libraries

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.720
that work in the browser.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:02.840
So for example, it
supports plotly and Altair.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:07.230
And those are supported via
the Shiny widgets package,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.199
but I'm not going to go
over those right now.

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:12.170
But just so you
know, to use those,

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:13.280
you don't use render plot.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.700
There's a different
function for those.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.130
We'll have examples
for that as well.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:23.300
So if we want to plot like an
actual data set instead of just

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.390
this fake histogram,
there's a great example data

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:30.260
set in a package
called palmerpenguins.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:35.285
And let's import that like
this from palmerpenguins import

00:02:35.285 --> 00:02:36.550
load_penguins.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:45.020
Now, well, one cool thing
that you can do with Shiny

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.940
live is that I can actually
execute this line of code.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:48.660
So I'm on a Mac.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.365
So I can hit command enter and
it'll execute in the terminal.

00:02:52.365 --> 00:02:54.740
On a Windows machine or Linux,
you'd press Control Enter,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.180
but I'll just run it like that.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.820
So this palmerpenguins
package it's just available.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:07.670
And if I run
load_penguins down here,

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:13.040
it'll run it in this Python
console and print it out here.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.990
So we can see that
this is a dataframe.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:19.310
It's got 344 rows and 8 columns.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:25.280
And it's got data about
various penguin specimens

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.645
that were in the wild.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.110
So let's say we want
to take this data set

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:39.770
and make a scatterplot of
some of the measurements

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:42.410
that we took of these penguins.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:44.742
So let's get rid of all
this, all this stuff here.

00:03:44.742 --> 00:03:46.200
We're going to do
this differently.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.575
And in fact, this
time we're also

00:03:47.575 --> 00:03:49.620
going to use the seaborne
package for plotting.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:54.670
So let's say import
seaborn as sns.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:57.460
And then I also think
we don't need numpy.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:58.810
We don't need matplotlib.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.510
All right, so this
time, we're going

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:05.780
to do a different plot here.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:11.328
So let's say we'll do
penguins equals load_penguins.

00:04:11.328 --> 00:04:13.120
And now we want to make
a plot out of this.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.315
So I actually happen to
have done this already.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.000
This is like the
cooking show where

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.080
you put the pan in one
oven and you take it out

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:25.860
of the other one.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.770
So this is the code
here for this plot.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:33.445
And let's add a couple
of other things.

00:04:36.208 --> 00:04:37.500
So in this case, I've created--

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.210
I've used seaborn to
create this plot object.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.240
And then I'm going to
return that plot object.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.140
Previously with matplotlib,
what I did was I just had it do

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.440
the plotting command instead of
returning a matplotlib figure

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:53.520
object.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.450
And that's another way
that you can do it.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:58.510
But in this case, I'm going to
have it return the plot object.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:04.860
So if I run this, it's going to
take a moment to load seaborn,

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.440
I think.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:09.350
And there we have
our scatterplot.

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:15.170
And this is a scatterplot for
the three different species

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:15.770
of penguins.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:20.690
So that's what's happening
here, hue is species.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:24.290
And we've also fit
these linear model lines

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:27.050
or these-- yeah,
linear model fit lines

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.760
to each subset of the data.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.500
Now the slider doesn't
actually do anything right now.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.110
So I could take it out
or I could figure out

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:37.710
what I want to do with it.

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:42.740
So let's say we just want to
look at one species at a time

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.730
instead of looking at
all of them all at once.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.810
So right now, we have
this slider input that

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:49.740
doesn't actually do anything.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:50.880
So let's get rid of that.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:53.480
And let's add something
that lets us select

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:54.990
particular species to look at.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:06:02.155
So let's say ui dot input, we
can do, how about input select.

00:06:02.155 --> 00:06:04.000
So it'll give us
a selection box.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.390
So we can say this is which
species you want to look at.

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:17.170
And let's say which
species is your favorite.

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:20.583
And then we can give
it a list of choices.

00:06:20.583 --> 00:06:22.000
So I'm just going
to the next line

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:29.710
and say, Adelie, I hope that's
how to pronounce it, Gentoo,

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:33.110
and Chinstrap.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:34.830
And then we can
put a comma there.

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.960
And another cool thing
about this online editor

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:38.400
is you can just
click this button

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:44.110
and it'll reformat
the code, in theory.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:45.040
There we go.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.570
I would expect it to be wide--

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:51.310
the editor be wide.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:52.990
So that gives us--

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:54.340
we'll rerun this application.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:58.390
And then we can select a
particular species of penguin.

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:03.910
And now, again, this is
not hooked up to anything.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:05.780
We've just added
the UI component.

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:10.220
So in order to make this
actually work, let's say--

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:12.000
let's take a subset
of this data.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:21.810
So we can say penguins
equals penguins where--

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:29.025
we can do it this way,
penguin species equals input.

00:07:31.802 --> 00:07:33.260
All right, so this
is going to take

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.690
a subset for just one species.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:38.910
And let's rerun this out.

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:44.250
So this is just showing us
the data for Adelie penguins.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:45.750
And if we change
it, it's showing us

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:49.920
the data for Gentoo
penguins and Chinstrap.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.370
